LSI MegaRAID SAS (MFI) miniport, PCI 1000:0060 (SAS 1078) and 1000:0079 (SAS 2108), the controllers that
QEMU emulates as megasas and megasas-gen2 and that Proxmox VE offers as the "MegaRAID SAS 8708EM2"

The driver (megasas.c) is new code, GPL-2.0-or-later, written against the legacy SCSI port
interface (scsiport) like the BusLogic, PVSCSI and MPT miniports.

Origin of the device interface
  Project:  OpenBSD
  Files:    sys/dev/ic/mfireg.h revision 1.52 (2022/09/16), sys/dev/ic/mfi.c and sys/dev/pci/mfi_pci.c
            (the 2024 tree): the start-up sequence and the command layout only
  URL:      https://github.com/openbsd/src/blob/master/sys/dev/ic/mfireg.h
  License:  ISC, notice kept in the header of megasas.c
  Used:     the register offsets, the firmware states and the doorbell values, the layouts of the frame
            header, the init frame and its queue description, the I/O, pass-through and direct command
            frames, the 64-bit scatter/gather element, the command and status numbers, and the order of the
            start-up steps (firmware to ready, init frame with the reply queue, polled). No code was copied:
            the OpenBSD driver is written for its own SCSI midlayer and DMA API.

Not used: the Linux driver megaraid_sas (GPL-2.0-only, not compatible with this tree), and any LSI or
Broadcom binary driver.

How it talks to the controller (behaviour found with QEMU's model): every command, reads and writes included, is a
pass-through frame for the logical drive with the CDB in it (QEMU's model reads the CDB of read/write frames from
the pass-through position, so the block address form cannot be used); the reply queue has as many entries as the
firmware has commands (a longer one is refused); the register BAR of a controller that sits above 4 GB is taken from
the BAR in configuration space, because the port reports a 64-bit BAR above 4 GB with its upper half cut off.

What it does not do: the 1078 "xscale" register layout (PCI 1000:0411 and others: not emulated, not
registered), the 2008 "skinny" layout, the controller information and configuration commands (the logical
drives are found by probing the targets), events (no hot plug), RAID management, MSI, more than one
logical unit per target, CDBs longer than 16 bytes, more than 33 scatter/gather elements per request.
The command limits the firmware reports are checked before they are used, every wait on the controller
is bounded, and the reply queue indices, the contexts and the sense length that the controller writes are
validated.
